WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.400
Take a young kitten.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.180
Close one eye for six days.

3
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:10.020
Then record from cells in its visual cortex, one at a time.

4
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.320
The closed eye can now drive only a fraction of the cells it drove before.

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.600
Now close both eyes instead.

6
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.640
The imbalance is far smaller.

7
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.180
Why would two closed eyes shift it less?

8
00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.390
Here is how that is measured.

9
00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:27.730
A fine electrode sits next to one cortical cell and records its spikes.

10
00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:30.910
A bar of light is shown to one eye, then the other.

11
00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.410
Each cell goes into one of seven groups.

12
00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.830
Group one answers only to one eye.

13
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.570
Group seven answers only to the other eye.

14
00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:41.470
Group four answers about equally to both.

15
00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:46.410
Count the cells in each group, and a normal cat gives a hump, with most cells in the

16
00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.410
middle groups.

17
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.590
Now shut one eye of a young kitten for six days.

18
00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.730
The closed eye can drive only a fraction of the cells it drove before.

19
00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.810
Most of the histogram moves toward the open eye.

20
00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.670
We call this a shift in ocular dominance.

21
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:05.770
A closure from birth for about three months left very few cells that the closed eye

22
00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:08.690
could drive. Yet its retina still responded.

23
00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:12.033
Is this disuse? Try it in kittens.

24
00:01:12.713 --> 00:01:17.433
If unused connections simply wither, closing both eyes should cause a large loss of

25
00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:18.433
responses to both.

26
00:01:18.913 --> 00:01:24.933
It did not. With both eyes shut, many cells still answered to each eye, though some

27
00:01:24.933 --> 00:01:26.553
were abnormal or silent.

28
00:01:27.113 --> 00:01:29.733
So the open eye is what hurts the closed one.

29
00:01:30.353 --> 00:01:34.673
The two eyes compete for the same cortex, and the more active eye takes territory

30
00:01:34.673 --> 00:01:35.673
from the other.

31
00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:41.473
Age matters. The same closure in an adult cat, even for months, left the histogram

32
00:01:41.473 --> 00:01:46.073
normal. In kittens, sensitivity began near the start of the fourth week.

33
00:01:46.593 --> 00:01:50.533
It stayed high for several weeks, then declined through the third month.

34
00:01:51.293 --> 00:01:56.653
A window like this, when the brain needs experience, is a critical period or a

35
00:01:56.653 --> 00:02:01.473
sensitive period. Critical periods need experience, and the effects last.

36
00:02:02.433 --> 00:02:05.293
Sensitive periods just make experience count more.

37
00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:08.373
This idea helps explain amblyopia.

38
00:02:09.073 --> 00:02:13.513
One eye sees poorly because the two eyes gave the young brain unequal input.

39
00:02:14.153 --> 00:02:16.513
Glasses alone do not immediately fix it.

40
00:02:16.913 --> 00:02:18.773
The treatment follows from competition.

41
00:02:19.553 --> 00:02:23.753
Cover the good eye with a patch, so the weak eye carries the useful images.

42
00:02:24.473 --> 00:02:25.953
It works best in young children.

43
00:02:26.473 --> 00:02:30.353
Keep the rule: the eyes compete, most easily when young.
